<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">British Cyprus</h1><p class="st-meta">1878 CE – 1960 CE · capital Nicosia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Cyprus under British rule from 1878 to 1960, a strategic base whose Greek Cypriot campaign for union with Greece led to independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus-convention">Cyprus Convention</a> of 1878, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> allowed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to occupy and administer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, while keeping formal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, in return for British support against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> became the first high commissioner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the island when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1914 and made it a crown colony in 1925.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A British governor ruled with a partly elected legislative council, abolished after the 1931 riots, when Government House was burned. The population, about four-fifths Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and one-fifth Turkish Muslim, was divided by the Greek demand for enosis, union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Agriculture, mining and later British military bases shaped the economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1955 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoka">EOKA</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> movement under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grivas">George Grivas</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makarios-iii">Archbishop Makarios</a> III, fought British rule. The Zurich and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> agreements of 1959 created the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, which became independent in August 1960, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> keeping two sovereign base areas.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-cyprus.jpg" alt="British Cyprus" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Shandris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cyprus_(1922%E2%80%931960).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1878 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nicosia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Garnet Wolseley, John Harding, Hugh Foot</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Turkish, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Cyprus, Cyprus Convention territory</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=british-cyprus" aria-label="British Cyprus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5525 906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Cyprus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5845 1162L5709 970M5845 1162L4997 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5845 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5845 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nicosia. <a href="/atlas?year=1919&amp;focus=british-cyprus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

